Track 2: "Where are the birds sleeping?"
- Story & Context: Clara, a girl who had been blind since birth, stood beneath the central pylon of the carnival and asked the musicians this haunting question while the world was at its darkest. The lead guitarist stopped his frantic palm-muting to answer with a melody so mournful it reportedly caused the local dogs to begin howling in harmony. The air smelled of ozone and damp velvet, a memory she would carry long after the sun returned. "The sky has no feathers tonight, only teeth."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A grainy, blue-tinted Polaroid style photo of a young girl reaching her hands toward a dark, swirling sky above a rusted, ornate carnival wheel.

Track 3: Refraction-Stress Fatigue
- Story & Context: The final song of the eclipse is played when the light begins to aggressively return to the landscape, creating a physical tension known as Refraction-Stress that vibrates the very bones of the audience. The band members have been known to collapse from exhaustion as the final banjo riff snaps a string, ending the set before the sun hits full strength. This track has become a local myth about a lost performer who supposedly vanished into the heat shimmer after the last chord sounded. "I am holding the crack, and the crack is holding me."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A surrealist painting of a human silhouette dissolving into jagged, multicolored shards of glass against a backdrop of a blinding, piercing-white desert morning sun.

